 \subsection{A smaller patch: single-column smoothing}
 \label{sec:single-column}
 
-The star patch of Section~\ref{sec:inner-solvers} spans two columns of
-cells.  On thin domains one might expect a single column to suffice:
-as $\varepsilon \to 0$ the vertical derivative dominates the
-divergence, so the divergence-free fields that a robust smoother must
-represent patch by patch~\cite{ArnoldHdivPC, PCPATCH} would appear to
-become fewer.  We test this with the single-column patch built on the
-closure of a base-mesh cell: one column of cells together with the
-horizontal-velocity degrees of freedom on its two vertical facets
-(\opt{firedrake.ASMVankaPC} with \opt{pc_vanka_construct_dim:1} in
-place of \opt{firedrake.ASMStarPC} in Appendix~\ref{app:petsc-options}).
-For $k = 2$ it has $18N_z + 4$ unknowns against $30N_z + 8$ for the
-star ($364$ against $608$ at $N_z = 20$), and its LU factor has
-$21\,060$ nonzeros against $53\,468$.  The question is whether this
-patch keeps the robustness of Sections~\ref{sec:robustness}
-and~\ref{sec:nonlinear}, and at what cost.
-
-The premise fails: in the Piola-mapped basis the discrete divergence
-matrix does not depend on the geometry at all, only the mass matrices
-do, so the kernel of the grad--div term is the same for every
-$\varepsilon$.  On the slice that kernel is $\nabla^\perp\psi$ for
-$\psi \in \mathrm{CG}_k(x) \otimes \mathrm{CG}_k(z)$ constant along the
-top and along the bottom, and a single column supports only the $\psi$
-built on the $x$-bubbles of its cell: $(k-1)(kN_z - 1)$ kernel
-functions per patch instead of $(2k-1)(kN_z - 1)$ for the star, and
-none for $k = 1$.  What rescues the single-column patch for $k = 2$ is
-the coarse level: $\mathrm{CG}_2$ on the fine base mesh is the direct
-sum of $\mathrm{CG}_2$ on the coarsened one and the fine cell bubbles,
-so the coarse-grid kernel and the single-column kernels span the whole
-kernel as a direct sum (on an $8 \times 3$ mesh, with Firedrake's
-prolongation, the kernel has dimension $81$ for $k = 2$ and the two
-parts have $41$ and $40$; for $k = 1$ they have $9$ and $0$ of $17$).
-The price is the constant of this splitting, which bounds the
-two-level additive method as $\delta \to 0$.  Separating vertical
-modes, which is exact here because every patch is a whole column, a
-divergence-free field of the $l$-th vertical mode has horizontal
-velocity $U(x)$ times the mode profile, $U \in \mathrm{CG}_2(x)$, and
-carries the energy $\|U\|^2 + \rho_l\,\Delta x^2\|U'\|^2$ with
-$\rho_l = mL_z^2/(\pi^2 l^2 \Delta x^2)$ (the discrete vertical
-wavenumber replacing $\pi l/L_z$), $m = 1 + \Delta t^2N^2/4$ as in
-Section~\ref{sec:theory}, and $\Delta x = L_x/(4N_x)$ the cell width
-of the finest level.  The constant of the split into coarse
-interpolant and bubbles grows from $2.0$ for $\rho_l \to \infty$ to
-$12.3$ for $\rho_l \to 0$ ($15.7$ with the three levels used here),
-while the corresponding constant of the star patch stays below $1.3$.
-Since $\rho_l$ decreases with $l$, the highest modes sit at the
-constant $12.3$ on every mesh; the gravest mode, which carries the
-test data (Remark~\ref{rem:thin-faster}), has
-$\rho_1 = mL_z^2/(\pi^2\Delta x^2)$, so thin, weakly stratified
-domains, $\rho_1 \to 0$, put that mode at the worst constant too: the
-least favourable case, the opposite of the motivation.
-
-The shift fixes the regime in which this matters.  With
-$\delta = K\,\delta_{\mathrm{thr}}$,
-$\delta_{\mathrm{thr}} = \Delta t/(2C_M^2)$ the admissible shift of
-Corollary~\ref{cor:dimensional}, the grad--div coefficient
-$\Delta t/\delta$ in~\eqref{eq:Schur-explicit} equals $2C_M^2/K$.  The
-admissible range $K \lesssim 1$ is therefore exactly the
-grad--div-dominated regime in which the kernel condition decides the
-robustness of the smoother, and the relevant test of the single-column
-patch is whether its iteration count stays bounded as $K \to 0$.  For
-$K \gg 1$ the grad--div term is weak and any smoother serves, but
-there the outer iteration itself degrades (Section~\ref{sec:robustness}),
-so agreement between the two patches in that range is no evidence.
-Table~\ref{tab:single-column-block} isolates the smoother: the
-velocity--buoyancy block alone is solved by FGMRES to a relative
-residual of $10^{-8}$, preconditioned by the three-level V-cycle of
-the solver (six GMRES iterations per level, preconditioned by the
-patch operator), for a random right-hand side on the nominal
-configuration.  With the star patch the V-cycle is close to an exact
-solver for both degrees.  With the single-column patch and $k = 2$
-the count is bounded as $K \to 0$ ($20$ at $K = 0.1$ against $17$ at
-$K = 1$), as the kernel condition met by the coarse level and the
-bubbles predicts, and it is bounded in every other parameter at
-$K = 1$: $17$, $17$, $16$, $9$, $6$ for $\Delta t = 1$, $50$, $10^3$,
-$10^4$, $10^5\,\mathrm{s}$; $11$ to $19$ for $N_x = 10$ to $80$; $16$
-to $17$ for $N_z = 10$ to $80$; and $16$ to $18$ for
-$L_z = 12\,\mathrm{km}$ down to $250\,\mathrm{m}$.  For $k = 1$, where
-the condition fails, the count grows as $K$ decreases and the solve
-does not converge within $200$ iterations at $K = 0.1$; in the full
-nonlinear solver at $K = 1$ and $\Delta t = 50\,\mathrm{s}$ the $k = 1$
-single-column patch needs $56$, $78$ and $99$ iterations for
-$N_x = 10$, $20$, $40$ against $11$, $13$, $13$ for the star, growing
-with the mesh.  The single-column patch is therefore a robust smoother
-for $k = 2$ only, and about eight times weaker than the star.
-
