+The star patch of Section~\ref{sec:inner-solvers}, the column patch
+of the preceding sections, spans the two columns of cells around a
+vertex of the base mesh.  A smaller patch is suggested by the
+geometry.  A robust patch smoother needs the divergence-free fields,
+the kernel of the grad--div term, to have a basis of functions each
+supported in one patch (Section~\ref{sec:inner-solvers}); as the
+aspect ratio decreases the domain becomes thinner and the divergence
+is dominated by its vertical derivative, so this kernel is expected to
+be captured within single columns of cells.  We therefore replace the
+vertex star on the multigrid levels by a single-column patch, the
+closure of one cell of the base mesh: one column of cells together
+with the horizontal-velocity degrees of freedom on its two vertical
+facets, obtained with \opt{firedrake.ASMVankaPC} and
+\opt{pc_vanka_construct_dim:1} in place of \opt{firedrake.ASMStarPC}
+and \opt{pc_star_construct_dim:0} (Appendix~\ref{app:petsc-options}).  For $k = 2$ it has $18N_z + 4$
+unknowns against $30N_z + 8$ for the star ($364$ against $608$ at
+$N_z = 20$).
+
+Figures~\ref{fig:single-column-linear} and~\ref{fig:single-column-nonlinear}
+show the four sweeps of Sections~\ref{sec:robustness}
+and~\ref{sec:nonlinear}, with the same shifts $\delta = C_1/\Delta t$,
+meshes, aspect ratios and tolerances, for both patches.  The
+single-column patch converges in every run in which the star patch
+converges, and at $\Delta t = 50\,\mathrm{s}$, the time step of the mesh
+sweeps, its counts depend as little on $\Delta x$, $\Delta z$ and
+$\varepsilon$ as those of the star patch.  For the linear problem the
+two patches give the same counts to within one iteration in $165$ of
+the $191$ runs in which both converge, among them every run of
+Figures~\ref{fig:robust-dx}--\ref{fig:robust-ar}; the single column
+needs clearly more only for the smallest shift, $C_1 = 10^{-5}$ (at
+most $7$ against $4$), and in thin domains at large time steps
+($L_z \le 2\,\mathrm{km}$, $\Delta t \ge 100\,\mathrm{s}$), up to $2.75$
+times the iterations ($11$ against $4$ at $L_z = 250\,\mathrm{m}$,
+$\Delta t = 500$--$1000\,\mathrm{s}$).  For the nonlinear problem the
+two patches fail in the same runs.  Where the shift lies above the
+worst-case admissible bound $\delta \lesssim \Delta t/(2C_M^2)$ of
+Corollary~\ref{cor:dimensional} ($\Delta t \le 100\,\mathrm{s}$ for
+$C_1 = 10^{-5}$) their counts are equal or close (median ratio $1.0$,
+at most $2.1$); inside the admissible range
+($\Delta t \ge 500\,\mathrm{s}$) the single column needs two to three
+times the iterations (median $2.6$, at most $5.9$), and its counts grow
+under vertical refinement (median $19$ at $N_z = 10$ and $38$ at
+$N_z = 80$, against $10$ for the star).  The smaller patch is therefore
+a working alternative to the star patch: in the linear problem and at
+the time step of the parameter sweeps it is as parameter-robust as the
+star patch, while in the grad--div-dominated regime of the nonlinear
+problem it needs two to three times the iterations.  Each outer
+iteration is about $30\%$ cheaper (measured for the nonlinear solver at
+$(N_x, N_z) = (20, 20)$), so that it is also faster wherever the two
+counts are within a factor of about $1.4$.  All runs use $k = 2$; for
+$k = 1$ a single column contains no divergence-free fields and the
+star patch is required.
 
