-For $k = 2$ the single-column patch is therefore a workable
-alternative to the star patch: within the admissible range of the
-shift it converges wherever the star patch does, its iteration count
-is bounded in $K$, $\Delta t$, $\Delta x$, $\Delta z$ and
-$\varepsilon$, and each iteration is cheaper.  Its drawbacks are a
-larger constant, worst precisely for the thin, weakly stratified
-domains that motivated it, where it costs up to ten times the
-iterations and four times the wall-clock time; a growth with $N_z$
-and a run-to-run variability in the full solver that the two-level
-constant does not account for; and no robustness at all for $k = 1$.
-It pays off at large time steps, $\rho_1 \gtrsim 10$ on the nominal
-geometry; the star patch remains the robust default.
